<TopicPage slug="subjects/international-relations/foundational/frameworks-for-understanding-international-relations">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    MIT's 17.41 and the textbook it is built around — Frieden, Lake &amp; Schultz's
    <em>World Politics: Interests, Interactions, Institutions</em> — reduce the chaos of
    world politics to three lenses. <strong>Interests</strong> are what actors want: power,
    security, wealth, ideology. <strong>Interactions</strong> are what happens when their
    choices collide: situations of cooperation or bargaining. <strong>Institutions</strong>
    are the rules that structure both — who may do what, with whom, and who verifies it.
    The method of the course is to take one real puzzle and look at it through each lens in
    turn, refusing the lazy answer that a single motive "explains" a war, a treaty or a
    trade dispute.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why a framework at all? Because world politics is both too big and too fast to be
    learned as a pile of events. Memorise every crisis of the twentieth century and you will
    still be unprepared for the next one; learn the recurring structures — who wants what,
    which situations repeat, which rules change the game — and every new crisis arrives
    pre-organised. The frameworks approach is the field's version of a mechanic's training:
    not a list of every car, but the small set of systems every car contains. The rest of
    this chapter builds the three lenses, then shows the discipline's own history as a fourth
    lens, then runs the toolkit on war itself.
  </p>

  <h2>How the rules got there</h2>
  <Widget no="1" caption="the slow construction of the international order — sovereignty, then institutions" widget="timeline" config={irTimeline} />
  <p>
    Read the timeline through the three lenses. Westphalia and Vienna set the deep rule —
    sovereignty and balance. The League, UN, GATT and WTO are attempts to build
    institutions that make cooperation enforceable where interests alone would leave states
    trapped in rivalry. The Paris Agreement shows the modern version of the problem:
    universal membership, minimal enforcement. The state itself is an institutional
    achievement, not a fact of nature: the map of 1500 (dynastic realms, empires, city
    leagues) looks nothing like today's because the sovereign-territorial state won a long
    competition and then wrote itself into the rules — territorial integrity and
    non-interference — that now protect it everywhere.
  </p>

  <h2>The three lenses, one at a time</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Interests:</strong> states (and firms, NGOs, voters) hold preferences over outcomes. Much apparent "irrationality" in foreign policy is just clashing interests — or clashing interests <em>inside</em> one government.</li>
    <li><strong>Interactions:</strong> some situations are coordination (everyone gains from agreeing on one standard); others are collective-action dilemmas (climate, arms races — each prefers to free-ride); most hard cases are bargaining over who gets how much of what.</li>
    <li><strong>Institutions:</strong> rules lower the cost of making and keeping agreements — they gather information, lengthen the shadow of the future, and raise the cost of defection. They have no world police, which is exactly why their design matters.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Each lens earns its place by explaining something the others cannot. Take a trade
    dispute. The <em>interests</em> lens says: industries and voters have different stakes,
    so a tariff serves some citizens at others' expense — the "national interest" is a
    coalition, not a single will. The <em>interactions</em> lens says: tariffs are a
    prisoner's dilemma — each country is better off protecting its own industries whatever
    the other does, though both would be better off with free trade — which is why trade
    wars are attractions, not accidents. The <em>institutions</em> lens says: the WTO
    exists precisely to change the payoff table — binding commitments and dispute panels
    let governments liberalise without being punished by import surges, converting the
    dilemma into cooperation. One dispute, three mechanisms, and a policy prescription
    (build rules) that no single lens would generate.
  </p>
  <p>
    Distinguishing the interaction types matters practically. In <strong>coordination</strong>
    problems (which side of the road to drive on; one standard plug), no one gains from
    defecting once an agreement exists, so institutions are easy — norms can carry them
    (though Schelling's insight stands: where several equilibria exist, the fight is over
    <em>which</em> one, and that fight can be fierce). In <strong>cooperation</strong>
    problems (arms control, fishing quotas, climate), each side gains from cheating while
    the other complies, so promises alone are worthless and monitoring, verification and
    repeated play do the work. Misdiagnosing a cooperation problem as a coordination problem
    — "just get everyone to agree" — is the classic error of naive diplomacy.
  </p>
  <p>
    The lenses also fix who counts as an actor. The state is the primary one —
    Westphalia's legacy — but the framework extends without strain to international
    organisations, firms, NGOs and militaries, each with interests of its own and each
    interacting through the same logic. And it scales: the same puzzle can be read at the
    level of the individual leader, the domestic coalition, or the international system.
    What the framework refuses is the single-actor shortcut of saying "France did X" —
    interests are held by someone in particular, and finding that someone is half the
    analysis.
  </p>

  <h2>The discipline's forgotten history — as a lens on itself</h2>
  <p>
    IR as an academic subject is usually dated to 1919, when the first chair of international
    politics was endowed at the University College of Wales, Aberystwyth, in memory of the
    war just ended. The standard textbook story then runs: a naive <em>idealist</em> interwar
    generation believed law and the League could abolish war; the catastrophes of the 1930s
    and the Second World War discredited them; <em>realism</em> (E. H. Carr's <em>The Twenty
    Years' Crisis</em>, 1939; Hans Morgenthau's <em>Politics Among Nations</em>, 1948)
    replaced wishful thinking with the analysis of power — the field's "first great debate".
  </p>
  <p>
    The word <em>forgotten</em> in this unit's title points at what that story leaves out.
    First, the chronology is wrong: systematic writing about international politics long
    predates 1919, and much of it was about <em>empire</em> — how to administer the world
    the great powers actually governed — alongside international law, diplomacy and trade.
    Scholars such as Brian Schmidt and David Long have shown that the "first great debate"
    was less a debate than a later construction, and that interwar "idealism" was a more
    varied and less naive body of work than the strawman version allows. Second, the field's
    founding institutions were creations of the Anglo-American establishment — the Royal
    Institute of International Affairs (Chatham House, 1920) and the Council on Foreign
    Relations — staffed by people drafting the <em>Pax Britannica</em> and then the
    <em>Pax Americana</em>. The questions a discipline asks, the forgotten history argues,
    reflect who is asking them: the "international" of early IR was largely the
    <em>inter-imperial</em>. Third, the era's counter-voices — the Pan-African congresses,
    W. E. B. Du Bois's writings on empire and peace — asked whether the state system itself
    was built on colonial domination; they were largely excluded from the field's canon and
    are only now being recovered. The lesson is methodological, not merely corrective:
    <strong>theories are products of their moment</strong>, and a disciplined IR reader asks
    of every framework (including the three-lens toolkit of this chapter) what problem, for
    whom, in whose world, it was built to solve.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "realism vs liberalism" is not the whole of IR theory,
  and neither is a description of how statesmen behave. They are analytical lenses with
  specified assumptions (realism: anarchy compels states to prioritise power and security;
  liberal institutionalism: interdependence and institutions change the game enough to
  sustain cooperation), each strong in its domain — realism explains why institutions are
  hard, institutionalism explains why they exist at all — and each silent where the other
  speaks. The frameworks approach holds both without pretending either is complete.</p>

  <h2>The sharpest tool: the bargaining model of war</h2>
  <Widget no="2" caption="war as bargaining failure — Fearon's logic, step by step" widget="equationStep" config={bargainingCfg} />
  <p>
    This is the frameworks approach at full power: a puzzle (why do costly wars happen when
    deals exist?), decomposed into interests (each wants the whole prize), interactions (a
    bargaining problem), and institutions (missing or failed ones). It also predicts where
    peace comes from: transparency that closes information gaps, and designs that make
    commitments credible.
  </p>
  <p>
    A concrete run-through: two states contest a province. War costs both sides dearly —
    soldiers, treasure, destruction — so any settlement that gives each side more than its
    expected share of the war's outcome exists, and rational states should find it. They
    fail for the two named reasons. <em>Private information with incentives to bluff</em>:
    each exaggerates its resolve (a bluff that might win the whole prize cheaply), so the
    other cannot tell where the true bargaining range lies — and only fighting
    <em>reveals</em> the truth, horribly. <em>Commitment problems</em>: even fully informed,
    a rising power cannot credibly promise not to use tomorrow's strength, and the declining
    power cannot trust tomorrow's promises — so it may rationally fight now, while fighting
    is still cheap. Institutions attack both mechanisms (verification regimes close
    information gaps; guarantees, alliances and constitutions make promises costly to break),
    which is why the model is simultaneously a theory of war and a theory of why
    institutions matter.
  </p>

  <h2>Applying the lenses: climate change</h2>
  <p>
    Take one modern puzzle and test the toolkit. <em>Interests:</em> every state prefers a
    stable climate, but each also prefers others to pay for it — divergent development
    levels make the split bitterly contested. <em>Interactions:</em> a classic
    collective-action problem, not a misunderstanding: abatement is costly to the actor and
    benefits everyone, so each state wants others' abatement and drags its own — Olson's
    logic of collective action, at planetary scale. <em>Institutions:</em> Paris 2015
    builds transparency (pledge-and-review) precisely to attack the information and
    commitment problems — weakly, and everyone knows it, which is the honest lesson of
    institutions: they shape outcomes only as strongly as their design and their members'
    interests allow. Notice how the framework also identifies what would change the
    outcome: technologies that make abatement an interest rather than a cost (then the
    cooperation problem dissolves into coordination — everyone races for the same cheap
    solution), or enforcement power that changes payoffs. The lenses do not just describe;
    they locate the levers.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three refinements take the toolkit to expert use. First, <strong>levels of analysis</strong>:
    any outcome can be read at the system level (the structure pushes two states into
    rivalry), the state/domestic level (coalitions, institutions, regime type), or the
    individual level (leaders' beliefs, misperception). The disciplined move is to say
    explicitly which level carries the explanation — "democratic peace" is a regime-level
    claim, the July crisis of 1914 reads best at all three at once — because the levels
    have different policy implications (system: change the structure; domestic: change the
    coalition; individual: change the man's information). Second, <strong>the shadow of the
    future</strong>: Axelrod's iterated prisoner's dilemma shows cooperation emerging among
    self-interested players when they expect to interact repeatedly (the <em>tit-for-tat</em>
    result), which grounds institutionalism in game theory rather than hope — and explains
    why cooperation is easier between neighbours who must live with each other than
    strangers who meet once. Third, <strong>institutions as power</strong>: rules are never
    neutral — the UN Security Council's veto, the IMF's weighted voting, the WTO's
    consensus process each bake in a distribution of power. Reading institutions through the
    forgotten-history lens (who designed them, for whose world) and the bargaining lens
    (what promises they make credible) together is the mature form of the discipline —
    the point at which the frameworks stop being a checklist and become a way of seeing.
  </p>
